<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!-- ============================================================
     Chapter: Reading Component Datasheets  (Day 5X)
     55-minute standalone class — no pre-class reading required.
     ============================================================ -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-io-datasheets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Reading Component Datasheets</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-datasheets-inclass">
        <title>In-Class: Datasheet Scavenger Hunt</title>

        <!-- ── Opening (≈5 min) ──────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-datasheets-intro">
            <title>Embedded Systems Need Peripherals</title>
            <p>
                A microcontroller on its own senses nothing and drives nothing.
                Every useful embedded system connects the MCU to the physical
                world through <em>input</em> and <em>output</em> components:
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-mcu-io-block">
                <caption>Top-level view of an embedded system.  Sensors,
                switches, and data streams feed into the MCU through interfaces;
                the MCU sends commands out to displays, actuators, and data
                streams through interfaces on the other side.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day05X-InputOutputDatasheets/slide04_img1.png"
                       width="75%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Getting a peripheral to work requires answers to many questions
                that only the component's <term>datasheet</term> can provide.
                What supply voltage does it need — and what are the minimum and
                maximum operating conditions it can tolerate?  What signal does
                it produce or consume, and what does that signal actually
                represent?  Does the chip do its own analog-to-digital
                conversion and give you numbers over I²C, or does it output a
                raw voltage that your MCU's ADC must interpret?  Is there a
                built-in analog front end, or do you need external signal
                conditioning?  How should you wire it up, and what does your
                code need to configure before it will respond?  These are a
                few of the essential questions — but not the only ones.
            </p>
            <p>
                Datasheets can be intimidating — they are written for engineers
                who already know the jargon, and they pack enormous amounts of
                information into dense tables.  But they follow a predictable
                structure.  Once you know where to look, you can navigate any
                datasheet quickly.
            </p>

            <insight xml:id="insight-datasheet-sections">
                <title>Every Datasheet Has the Same Structure</title>
                <p>
                    The first page always gives the high-level picture:
                    <em>Features</em> (a bullet list of what the part does)
                    and a <em>Description</em> paragraph (always read this —
                    it names the interface and application).  The body then
                    contains, roughly in this order:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Pinout / Pin descriptions</em> — which pin is which,
                    and what each one does.
                    <em>Absolute Maximum Ratings</em> — never exceed these;
                    doing so destroys the part.
                    <em>Recommended Operating Conditions</em> — the range
                    the part is designed to work in.
                    <em>Electrical Characteristics</em> — voltages, currents,
                    timing, in a table with Min / Typ / Max columns.
                    <em>Application Information</em> (sometimes called
                    "Theory of Operation" or "Detailed Description") —
                    the most important section for a designer: example
                    schematics, register maps, and usage notes.
                    <em>Packaging Information</em> — physical dimensions
                    of the available package options (DIP, SOT-23, QFN,
                    etc.).  Rarely relevant for breadboard work, but
                    critical if you are designing a PCB.
                </p>
                <p>
                    One notation to watch for: a pin name with a bar over it
                    (or a star next to it in a table) is <em>active low</em>
                    — you pull it to ground to activate it, rather than
                    driving it high.
                </p>
            </insight>
        

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-peripherals" ref="fig-mcu-io-block">
                <ul>
                    <li>A microcontroller on its own senses nothing and drives nothing.</li>
                    <li>Every useful system connects the MCU to the world through input and output components.</li>
                    <li>Sensors, switches, and data streams feed in; the MCU drives displays, actuators, and data streams out.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Inputs feed the MCU through interfaces; the MCU sends commands out through interfaces on the other side.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-questions">
                <ul>
                    <li>What supply voltage does it need — and what min/max conditions can it tolerate?</li>
                    <li>What signal does it produce or consume, and what does that signal represent?</li>
                    <li>Does it do its own conversion and give you numbers over I²C, or output a raw voltage your ADC must interpret?</li>
                    <li>How do you wire it up, and what must your code configure before it responds?</li>
                    <li>Datasheets are dense and written for experts — but they follow a predictable structure. Learn where to look and you can navigate any of them.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-skeleton">
                <ul>
                    <li>First page — Features (what it does) and Description (always read this — names the interface and application).</li>
                    <li>Pinout / Pin descriptions — which pin is which.</li>
                    <li>Absolute Maximum Ratings — never exceed; doing so destroys the part.</li>
                    <li>Recommended Operating Conditions — the range it’s designed for.</li>
                    <li>Electrical Characteristics — voltages, currents, timing, with Min / Typ / Max columns.</li>
                    <li>Application Information — the most important section for a designer: example schematics, register maps, usage notes.</li>
                    <li>Packaging — physical dimensions (rarely needed for breadboards).</li>
                    <li>Watch for active-low: a bar over a pin name (or a star in a table) means you pull it to ground to activate it.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
            </subsection>

        <!-- ── Scavenger Hunt Activity (≈45 min) ─────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-datasheets-scavenger">
            <title>Datasheet Scavenger Hunt</title>
            <p>
                Your table will be assigned one component.  Open its
                datasheet from Canvas, display it on your table's monitor,
                and complete the Datasheet Worksheet below.  Each table will
                share their answers with the class at the end.
            </p>
            <p>
                The components, one per table:
                the TMP235 (analog temperature sensor),
                the DRV5053 (analog Hall-effect magnetic sensor),
                the DS3231 (I2C real-time clock),
                the LIS3DH (I2C or SPI three-axis accelerometer),
                and the Si7021 (I2C humidity and temperature sensor).
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-datasheet-passport">
                <title>The Datasheet Worksheet</title>

                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Work through Parts A–D, then build your group slide
                        in Part E.  Some questions have two versions
                        depending on whether your component is analog or
                        digital — answer the one that applies.
                        If you finish early, sketch a circuit on the board
                        showing how you would connect your component to the
                        STM32 Nucleo.
                    </p>
                </introduction>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-a">
                    <title>Part A — What Is It?</title>
                    <p><em>These answers are on the first page.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Read the <em>Description</em> (not the
                                Features list).  In one sentence, what does
                                this component do?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the <em>Recommended Operating
                                Conditions</em>.  What supply voltage range
                                (VDD or VCC) does the part accept?  Is
                                3.3 V within that range?
                                (For passive components with no supply pin,
                                find the maximum rated operating voltage
                                and power dissipation instead.)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the <em>Absolute Maximum Ratings</em>
                                table.  What is the maximum voltage the
                                part can survive?  Are there any other
                                limits listed that a careless user might
                                accidentally exceed?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-b">
                    <title>Part B — How Does It Communicate?</title>
                    <p><em>These answers are in the Pinout and Application sections.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What interface does this component use?
                                Check one (or more if it supports multiple):
                                <em>Passive (variable resistance)</em>,
                                <em>Analog voltage output</em>,
                                <em>I²C</em>,
                                <em>SPI</em>,
                                <em>UART/serial</em>,
                                <em>PWM input</em>,
                                <em>Other</em>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                List every pin needed for a minimal hookup.
                                For each pin, write its name and what it
                                connects to (VDD, GND, a specific STM32
                                pin, etc.).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-c">
                    <title>Part C — What Does It Output or Require?</title>
                    <p><em>These answers are in the Electrical Characteristics table.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <em>If your component is a passive sensor
                                (variable resistance):</em>
                                What is the resistance at the minimum and
                                maximum of its measurement range?  Is the
                                relationship between resistance and the
                                measured quantity linear, or does the
                                datasheet give a curve or formula?
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                <em>If your component has an analog voltage
                                output:</em>
                                What is the output voltage at the minimum
                                and maximum of its measurement range?
                                Is the relationship linear?  Write the
                                transfer function — the equation that
                                converts output voltage to a physical value
                                (temperature, light level, etc.).
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                <em>If your component uses I²C:</em>
                                What address(es) can the part use?
                                Many parts have a fixed base address with
                                one or two address pins (A0, A1) that let
                                you set the last bits, so multiple copies
                                of the same chip can share a bus.  List
                                the possible addresses.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                <em>If your component uses SPI:</em>
                                What SPI mode does it require (clock
                                polarity CPOL and clock phase CPHA)?
                                What is the maximum clock frequency it
                                supports?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <!-- Part C is split across two tasks so that each one fits on
                     its projected slide with its writing room intact. As a
                     single task it ran 68px past the bottom of the frame and
                     the current question was clipped. -->
                <task xml:id="task-passport-c2">
                    <title>Part C, continued — Current and Power</title>
                    <p><em>Also in the Electrical Characteristics table.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What is the maximum current the part draws
                                from its supply?  Does that rule out
                                powering it from a GPIO pin (max 25 mA)?
                                (For passive components with no supply pin,
                                find the maximum power dissipation rating
                                instead.)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-d">
                    <title>Part D — Making It Work</title>
                    <p><em>These answers are in the Application Information section.</em></p>

                    <p><ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Find the example schematic or application
                                circuit.  Sketch the minimal hookup on the
                                board at your table —
                                just power, ground, and signal lines,
                                labeled with pin names.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Name one thing you would need to configure
                                or calculate in code before you could
                                read data from this component.  (Examples:
                                set the I²C address, choose a gain setting,
                                apply a conversion formula to the raw
                                ADC reading.)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                What was the most confusing or surprising
                                thing you found in the datasheet?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>

                <task xml:id="task-passport-slide">
                    <title>Part E — Group Slide</title>
                    <p>
                        Add one slide for your component to the shared class
                        deck (link on Canvas).  Include your answers to all
                        of Parts A–D — everything except the board sketch.
                        Keep it concise: use short phrases, not paragraphs.
                        Other groups will use this as a reference during the
                        share-out.
                    </p>
                </task>

            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-datasheet-worked">
                <p>
                    A worked Part E, from the year this ran: one table's slide on
                    the TMP23x analog temperature sensor, reproduced as they wrote
                    it.  It is the calibration for <q>concise</q> — short phrases,
                    five lines, no paragraphs.
                </p>
                <p><ul>
                    <li><em>Name, model, manufacturer.</em>  TMP23x temperature
                        sensor, Texas Instruments.</li>
                    <li><em>Description.</em>  Linear analog output; voltage
                        proportional to temperature over −40 °C to 150 °C, at
                        10 mV per °C.</li>
                    <li><em>How to connect it.</em>  Output to an ADC input on
                        the microcontroller; may want a series resistor and
                        capacitor to filter noise, depending on what the ADC
                        input needs.</li>
                    <li><em>How to communicate with it.</em>  There is no
                        protocol — the output <em>is</em> a voltage, so reading
                        it means converting it.</li>
                    <li><em>Anything else crucial.</em>  Which pins can do
                        analog: A0–A5, D10–D13, D4.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p>
                    Two things that answer is doing right, and are worth naming
                    at the share-out.  It gives the transfer function as a
                    number a program can use — 10 mV per °C — rather than
                    <q>outputs a voltage</q>.  And its last line is about
                    <em>our</em> board, not the component: the group checked
                    which STM32 pins could receive the signal.  That is Part D's
                    question in practice.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Parts A–D are component-specific, and their answers come out
                    of each table's own datasheet rather than from here.  The
                    worksheet already names the section each answer is in, which
                    is the part that transfers: <em>Description</em> and
                    <em>Recommended Operating Conditions</em> on the first pages,
                    <em>Absolute Maximum Ratings</em>, the pinout, <em>Electrical
                    Characteristics</em>, and <em>Application Information</em>
                    about halfway through.
                </p>
            </instructor>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-datasheet-key">
                <p>
                    Parts A–D, for the five components whose datasheets are in
                    the repository (<c>assets/datasheets/</c>).  Every number
                    below is cited to the table it came from, because that is the
                    habit Part A–D is teaching and the tables move between
                    revisions.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part A2 and A3 at a glance.</term>  Every one of the
                    five accepts 3.3 V, which is the answer to A2 in each case —
                    but the interesting column is the last one, because two of
                    these parts are destroyed by 5 V and three are not.
                </p>
                <tabular top="major" left="minor" bottom="minor">
                    <row bottom="major">
                        <cell>Part</cell><cell>Supply range (A2)</cell>
                        <cell>Absolute max supply (A3)</cell><cell>Survives 5 V?</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row><cell>TMP235</cell><cell>2.3–5.5 V (§6.3)</cell>
                        <cell>+6 V (§6.1)</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>DS3231</cell><cell>2.3–5.5 V (Recommended Operating Conditions)</cell>
                        <cell>−0.3 to +6.0 V on any pin</cell><cell>yes</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>DRV5053-Q1</cell><cell>2.7–38 V (§6.3)</cell>
                        <cell>−22 to +40 V (§6.1)</cell><cell>yes, easily</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Si7021-A20</cell><cell>1.9–3.6 V (Table 1)</cell>
                        <cell>+4.2 V (Table 7)</cell><cell><em>no</em></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="major"><cell>LIS3DH</cell><cell>1.71–3.6 V (Table 6)</cell>
                        <cell>+4.8 V (Table 9)</cell><cell><em>no</em></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <p>
                    That column is worth making the room say out loud.  A student
                    who has only ever seen 5 V parts will reach for the 5 V rail
                    out of habit, and on two of these five that is a destroyed
                    component rather than a wrong reading.  It is also the reason
                    the Absolute Maximum table is a separate question from the
                    Recommended Operating Conditions table: the first says what
                    kills it, the second says where it works.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part C2, supply current.</term>  None of the five comes
                    close to the 25 mA a GPIO pin can source, so the answer to
                    <q>does that rule out powering it from a pin</q> is no in
                    every case: TMP235 9 µA typical (§6.5), LIS3DH 11 µA in
                    normal mode at 50 Hz (Table 6), Si7021 150 µA while
                    converting but <em>4 mA peak</em> during power-up and I2C
                    activity (Table 2), DS3231 200 µA active at 3.63 V, and
                    DRV5053 2.7 mA typical (§6.5) — the largest of the five, and
                    still a tenth of the budget.  The honest follow-up is that
                    <q>it fits</q> is not the same as <q>it is a good idea</q>:
                    the Si7021's peak is fifteen times its running current, and
                    a pin that also has to stay at a clean logic level is not the
                    place to put that.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part C, worked for the analog branch: the TMP235.</term>
                    The transfer function is in §6.5: the sensor gain is
                    10 mV/°C and the output at 0 °C is 500 mV, both for the
                    TMP235 specifically — the TMP236 in the same document is
                    19.5 mV/°C and 400 mV, which is the first place a table
                    reader goes wrong.  So
                </p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
V_OUT(mV)  =  500  +  10 x T(degC)          T(degC)  =  (V_OUT(mV) - 500) / 10
]]></code></program>
                <p>
                    At the ends of the −40 °C to +150 °C range that is 100 mV and
                    2.000 V, so the whole output swing sits inside our 3.3 V ADC
                    range with room to spare and needs no divider.  It is linear:
                    §6.5 gives output nonlinearity as ±0.5 °C, which is a
                    deviation from the straight line rather than a curve to look
                    up.  One count of our 12-bit ADC is 3.3 V / 4096 = 0.806 mV,
                    which is 0.08 °C — the converter is roughly thirty times
                    finer than the sensor's own ±2.5 °C accuracy, and that
                    comparison is the one worth drawing.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part C, worked for the I2C branch: the Si7021.</term>
                    §5 gives the 7-bit base address as <c>0x40</c>, and Table 10
                    shows all seven bits fixed — there are no address pins, so
                    two of these cannot share a bus.  Contrast the LIS3DH, whose
                    address is <c>0011000b</c> with the SDO/SA0 pin selecting the
                    low bit: 0x18 with SA0 grounded, 0x19 with it tied to the
                    supply (§6.1.1), which is exactly the <q>list the possible
                    addresses</q> the worksheet asks for.  The DS3231's is
                    <c>1101000b</c>, 0x68, fixed.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Part D, the code question.</term>  For an analog part
                    it is the conversion: the ADC gives a count, and something
                    has to turn it into millivolts and then into °C or mT.  For a
                    digital part it is a command sequence — the Si7021 needs
                    <q>measure temperature</q> (0xE3 or 0xF3, Table 11) sent
                    before anything can be read back, and the 16-bit code that
                    comes back is not a temperature until it has been through the
                    equation in §5.1.2.  Both are the same lesson in two
                    dialects: the number on the wire is never the physical
                    quantity.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>The one that rewards a careful reader: the DRV5053.</term>
                    Its output is 0.2 V to 1.8 V with about 1 V at zero field
                    (§6.7), so the quantity it measures is <em>signed</em> — north
                    and south push the output opposite ways from a 1 V rest
                    point — while the ADC only ever returns a non-negative count.
                    A student who reports <q>output 0.2 to 1.8 V</q> and stops has
                    answered C correctly and missed what makes it hard.  Its
                    sensitivity is also not one number: the part comes in
                    variants from −11 to −90 mV/mT (and two positive ones), so
                    <q>the sensitivity</q> depends on which suffix is printed on
                    the package in front of them.
                </p>
            </instructor>
        

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-assign">
                <ul>
                    <li>Open its datasheet from Canvas, display it on your table’s monitor, and complete the Datasheet Worksheet (next slides).</li>
                    <li>Each table will share their answers with the class at the end.</li>
                    <li>The components, one per table: TMP235 (analog temperature), DRV5053 (analog Hall effect), DS3231 (I2C real-time clock), LIS3DH (I2C accelerometer), Si7021 (I2C humidity and temperature).</li>
                    <li>Some questions have analog and digital versions — answer the one that applies. Finish early? Sketch the hookup to the STM32 on your board.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
            </subsection>

        <!-- ── Share-out (≈5 min) ────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-datasheets-shareout">
            <title>Share-Out</title>
            <p>
                Each table presents their slide in 30 seconds or less.
                Cover at least these three things:
            </p>
            <p><ol>
                <li><p>Component name and what it does (one sentence).</p></li>
                <li><p>Interface type: passive, analog, I²C, SPI, or other.</p></li>
                <li><p>One thing that surprised you or that others should know.</p></li>
            </ol></p>
            <p>
                As you listen, note which components use analog interfaces
                and which use digital ones.  You will use several of these
                parts later in the course — this is your first look at them.
            </p>
        

            <slide xml:id="sl-datasheet-shareout">
                <ul>
                    <li>Each table presents their slide in 30 seconds or less. Cover at least:</li>
                    <li>Component name and what it does (one sentence).</li>
                    <li>Interface type: passive, analog, I²C, SPI, or other.</li>
                    <li>One thing that surprised you or that others should know.</li>
                    <li>As you listen, note which components are analog and which are digital — you’ll use several later in the course.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
            </subsection>

    </section>

</chapter>
